    "content": "Costco is facing a class-action lawsuit for allegedly violating California’s Automatic Renewal Law by failing to provide proper, timely notice before auto-renewing memberships. The suit claims Costco sent renewal notices too early (60 days prior) and failed to include required cancellation details, preventing members from easily canceling.Key Details of the Lawsuit:Plaintiff and Location: Filed in March 2026 by California resident Russel George II, representing a class of members.Alleged Violations: California law mandates companies provide clear, conspicuous notice 15–45 days before a subscription renews. The lawsuit alleges Costco sent notice 60 days ahead, violating this timeframe.Key Issues: The complaint argues that timely notice was not provided, preventing members from canceling. It also states the notice lacked crucial information regarding the renewal price and how to cancel, contradicting California's requirement that cancellation must be as easy as enrollment.Potential Outcome: If found at fault, Costco may be required to change its renewal notification process for all members, not just those in California.The lawsuit demands a jury trial, along with declaratory and injunctive relief. The case is part of a growing trend of consumer lawsuits targeting auto-renewal practices.",
